<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hakata</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hakata is a historic port on Hakata Bay in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, now a ward of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka">Fukuoka</a>. For centuries it was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s main gateway for trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, and the Mongol invasions of 1274 and 1281 landed nearby. Its merchants prospered in the Ming tally trade under the Ōuchi, and in 1601 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuroda-nagamasa">Kuroda Nagamasa</a> began Fukuoka castle beside the merchant town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hakata.jpg" alt="Hakata" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ZENsan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hakata_Port_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
